KG42.register("nodes-384b2b35ee72d49c667c3603",{"id":"COMP-eeda8c162516d608","code":"PRC046","label":"Purchase and sale pricing using grid-price midpoints and surplus-deficit weights","definition":"Unit sale price p_s and unit purchase price p_b are formed according to net surplus or shortfall: when E_s=0 and E_d=0, p_s=p_b=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2; when E_s>0, p_b=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2, p_s=(p_b∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}+p_{g,b}E_s)/∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}; when E_d>0, p_s=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2, p_b=(p_s∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}+p_{g,s}E_d)/∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}.","layer":"L6","template_code":"CBA","concept_id":"pricing.local","concept_ids":["pricing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: P2P unit sale price p_s and unit purchase price p_b.","Core operation: arithmetic midpoint of the grid purchase and sale prices and energy-weighted conversion of revenues and expenditures from surplus/shortfall transactions.","Conditional relationship: select the corresponding formula according to supply-demand balance, net surplus, or net shortfall.","Output: p_s and p_b under each case."]},"exclusions":["p_{g,s} is the grid sale price, p_{g,b} is the grid purchase price, and p_{g,s}>p_{g,b}.","E_s=∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}−∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}，E_d=−E_s","N_s and N_b are used to aggregate seller surplus and buyer shortfall, respectively.","Both P2P prices lie in [p_{g,b},p_{g,s}].","Peer-to-peer transactions require p_b\u003cp_{g,s} and p_s>p_{g,b}.","Prices must encourage participation and make the distribution of attainable gains satisfy the coalition core requirements."],"members":[{"id":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The P2P trading price must always encourage prosumers to participate. Scope: Participation incentives for the canonical coalition game's community P2P trading scheme. P2P trading is expected to be conducted exclusively by network prosumers, with negligible grid influence. Scope: Expected participant composition of the described P2P energy networks. Prosumers need to participate continuously. Scope: Sustainable operation of P2P trading within an energy system. Condition: To ensure sustainable P2P trading operation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Expected participant composition of the described P2P energy networks. | Participation incentives for the canonical coalition game's community P2P trading scheme. | Sustainable operation of P2P trading within an energy system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g03-e002"]},"constraint_refs":["30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"47d0486c971c20be9c0536771353683d5ca4e2abc2485a54d2e3f889800e69e8","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"30-motivational_game::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The mid-market pricing scheme sets both P2P buying and selling prices within [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. Scope: P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. Mid-market pricing provides a P2P buying price p_b below the grid selling price p_{g,s} and a P2P selling price p_s above the grid buying price p_{g,b}. Scope: Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. Condition: For peer-to-peer trades between prosumers with energy surplus and prosumers buying that energy. The P2P trading price must ensure that each prosumer's attainable revenue lies within the game's core. Scope: The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game. Both P2P prices equal the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices: p_s = p_b = (p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2. Scope: Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s = 0 and E_d = 0: total available prosumer surplus equals total demand. The buying price p_b remains the Scenario 1 mid-market rate. The selling price p_s equals revenue from peer demand priced at p_b plus residual CPS sales priced at p_{g,b}, divided by total prosumer surplus. Scope: Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s > 0: total available prosumer surplus exceeds total demand. The selling price p_s equals the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices. The buying price p_b equals the cost of peer surplus priced at p_s plus residual CPS purchases priced at p_{g,s}, divided by total prosumer demand. Scope: Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_d > 0: total available prosumer surplus is smaller than total demand.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. | Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. | Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]},"constraint_refs":["30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C005","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C006","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C007","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C008"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"32d518c38d8438cebcb543a957a394b6b84dcc7929eed258e0b3512aecd1de0a","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"30-motivational_game::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["30-motivational_game"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Stability of a coalition; Definition 2","anchor_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the w grand coalition2 is stable","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 5; 3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. This ﬁgure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and sellin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Fig. 5. This figure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and selling price always within the range [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. The value of p_{g,b} and p_{g,s} are chosen according to the electricity price in Brisbane, Australia.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Fig. 5. This figure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and selling price always within the range [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. The value of p_{g,b} and p_{g,s} are chosen according to the electricity price in Brisbane, Australia."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The mid-market rate is used for P2P pricing. Evidently, as shown in Fig. 5, using mid-market rate always allows prosumers to buy energy from other prosumers with energy surplus as a price p_b that is lower than the grid’s selling price p_{g,s} per unit of electricity. On the other hand, by participating in the P2P trading, prosumers with energy surplus can sell their energy to other prosumers at a price p_s > p_{g,b}. This pricing ensures that the revenue distribution that each prosumer achieve for participating in P2P trading lies within the core of the game.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The mid-market rate is used for P2P pricing. Evidently, as shown in Fig. 5, using mid-market rate always allows prosumers to buy energy from other prosumers with energy surplus as a price p_b that is lower than the grid’s selling price p_{g,s} per unit of electricity. On the other hand, by participating in the P2P trading, prosumers with energy surplus can sell their energy to other prosumers at a price p_s > p_{g,b}. This pricing ensures that the revenue distribution that each prosumer achieve for participating in P2P trading lies within the core of the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Stability of a coalition; Definition 2","anchor_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the w grand coalition2 is stable","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.1. Scenario 1 (E_s = 0, E_d = 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to their demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, do not need to trade any electricity with the CPS. According to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price (both selling p_s and buying price p_b) is p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"3.2.3.1. Scenario 1 (E_s = 0, E_d = 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to their demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, do not need to trade any electricity with the CPS. According to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price (both selling p_s and buying price p_b) is p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.2. Scenario 2 (E_s > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is greater than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, can not only meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency but can also sell a part of it to the CPS. Now, according to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price buying price p_b is as same as that in scenario 1. However, due to selling to the CPS, the P2P selling price p_s per unit of electricity becomes p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}}.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"3.2.3.2. Scenario 2 (E_s > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is greater than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, can not only meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency but can also sell a part of it to the CPS. Now, according to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price buying price p_b is as same as that in scenario 1. However, due to selling to the CPS, the P2P selling price p_s per unit of electricity becomes p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.3. Scenario 3 (E_d > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is smaller than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, cannot meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency and therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the P2P buying and selling prices become p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} and p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} respectively.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"3.2.3.3. Scenario 3 (E_d > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is smaller than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, cannot meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency and therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the P2P buying and selling prices become p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} and p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The monetary benefit ν to the group of prosumers can be viewed as the difference between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = p_{g,b}[E_s]^{+} − p_{g,s}[E_d]^{+}, where E_s = ∑_{n∈N_s} E_{n,s} − ∑_{n∈N_b} E_{n,d}, E_d = −E_s, [·]^{+} = max(0, ·), and p_{g,s} and p_{g,b} are respectively selling and buying prices by the grid.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The monetary benefit ν to the group of prosumers can be viewed as the difference between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = p_{g,b}[E_s]^{+} − p_{g,s}[E_d]^{+}, where E_s = ∑_{n∈N_s} E_{n,s} − ∑_{n∈N_b} E_{n,d}, E_d = −E_s, [·]^{+} = max(0, ·), and p_{g,s} and p_{g,b} are respectively selling and buying prices by the grid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Now, note that p_{g,s} > p_{g,b} [12], which is due to the fact that retailers pay prosumers less for their excess generation, since it is considered non-dispatchable. Therefore, a suitable P2P trading price can potentially motivate the prosumers to cooperate with one another in order to trade electricity among themselves, if possible, instead of trading with the CPS. To this end, the mid-market rate [57] is proposed to use as the pricing mechanism for this coalition game based P2P trading.","paper":"30-motivational_game","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Now, note that p_{g,s} > p_{g,b} [12], which is due to the fact that retailers pay prosumers less for their excess generation, since it is considered non-dispatchable. Therefore, a suitable P2P trading price can potentially motivate the prosumers to cooperate with one another in order to trade electricity among themselves, if possible, instead of trading with the CPS. To this end, the mid-market rate [57] is proposed to use as the pricing mechanism for this coalition game based P2P trading."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["mid_market_residual_weighted_rates"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L6-9b5459eb5f","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L6","ontological_kind":"mid_market_residual_weighted_rates"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"30-motivational_game::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","paper_id":"30-motivational_game","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The P2P trading price must always encourage prosumers to participate. Scope: Participation incentives for the canonical coalition game's community P2P trading scheme. P2P trading is expected to be conducted exclusively by network prosumers, with negligible grid influence. Scope: Expected participant composition of the described P2P energy networks. Prosumers need to participate continuously. Scope: Sustainable operation of P2P trading within an energy system. Condition: To ensure sustainable P2P trading operation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Expected participant composition of the described P2P energy networks. | Participation incentives for the canonical coalition game's community P2P trading scheme. | Sustainable operation of P2P trading within an energy system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g03-e002"]},"constraint_refs":["30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"47d0486c971c20be9c0536771353683d5ca4e2abc2485a54d2e3f889800e69e8"},{"id":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The mid-market pricing scheme sets both P2P buying and selling prices within [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. Scope: P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. Mid-market pricing provides a P2P buying price p_b below the grid selling price p_{g,s} and a P2P selling price p_s above the grid buying price p_{g,b}. Scope: Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. Condition: For peer-to-peer trades between prosumers with energy surplus and prosumers buying that energy. The P2P trading price must ensure that each prosumer's attainable revenue lies within the game's core. Scope: The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game. Both P2P prices equal the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices: p_s = p_b = (p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2. Scope: Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s = 0 and E_d = 0: total available prosumer surplus equals total demand. The buying price p_b remains the Scenario 1 mid-market rate. The selling price p_s equals revenue from peer demand priced at p_b plus residual CPS sales priced at p_{g,b}, divided by total prosumer surplus. Scope: Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s > 0: total available prosumer surplus exceeds total demand. The selling price p_s equals the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices. The buying price p_b equals the cost of peer surplus priced at p_s plus residual CPS purchases priced at p_{g,s}, divided by total prosumer demand. Scope: Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_d > 0: total available prosumer surplus is smaller than total demand.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. | Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. | Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]},"constraint_refs":["30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C005","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C006","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C007","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C008"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"32d518c38d8438cebcb543a957a394b6b84dcc7929eed258e0b3512aecd1de0a"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The P2P trading price must always encourage prosumers to participate. Scope: Participation incentives for the canonical coalition game's community P2P trading scheme.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Stability of a coalition; Definition 2","anchor_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the w grand coalition2 is stable","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The mid-market pricing scheme sets both P2P buying and selling prices within [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. Scope: P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism.","expression":"p_{g,b} \\le p_s \\le p_{g,s} \\;\\land\\; p_{g,b} \\le p_b \\le p_{g,s}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 5; 3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. This ﬁgure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and sellin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Fig. 5. This figure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and selling price always within the range [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. The value of p_{g,b} and p_{g,s} are chosen according to the electricity price in Brisbane, Australia."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Mid-market pricing provides a P2P buying price p_b below the grid selling price p_{g,s} and a P2P selling price p_s above the grid buying price p_{g,b}. Scope: Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. Condition: For peer-to-peer trades between prosumers with energy surplus and prosumers buying that energy.","expression":"p_b \u003c p_{g,s} \\;\\land\\; p_s > p_{g,b}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The mid-market rate is used for P2P pricing. Evidently, as shown in Fig. 5, using mid-market rate always allows prosumers to buy energy from other prosumers with energy surplus as a price p_b that is lower than the grid’s selling price p_{g,s} per unit of electricity. On the other hand, by participating in the P2P trading, prosumers with energy surplus can sell their energy to other prosumers at a price p_s > p_{g,b}. This pricing ensures that the revenue distribution that each prosumer achieve for participating in P2P trading lies within the core of the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The P2P trading price must ensure that each prosumer's attainable revenue lies within the game's core. Scope: The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Stability of a coalition; Definition 2","anchor_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the w grand coalition2 is stable","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Both P2P prices equal the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices: p_s = p_b = (p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2. Scope: Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s = 0 and E_d = 0: total available prosumer surplus equals total demand.","expression":"(E_s = 0 \\;\\land\\; E_d = 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_s","param_E_d"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.1. Scenario 1 (E_s = 0, E_d = 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to their demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, do not need to trade any electricity with the CPS. According to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price (both selling p_s and buying price p_b) is p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The buying price p_b remains the Scenario 1 mid-market rate. The selling price p_s equals revenue from peer demand priced at p_b plus residual CPS sales priced at p_{g,b}, divided by total prosumer surplus. Scope: Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s > 0: total available prosumer surplus exceeds total demand.","expression":"(E_s > 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left( p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} \\;\\land\\; p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}} \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_s","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.2. Scenario 2 (E_s > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is greater than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, can not only meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency but can also sell a part of it to the CPS. Now, according to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price buying price p_b is as same as that in scenario 1. However, due to selling to the CPS, the P2P selling price p_s per unit of electricity becomes p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The selling price p_s equals the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices. The buying price p_b equals the cost of peer surplus priced at p_s plus residual CPS purchases priced at p_{g,s}, divided by total prosumer demand. Scope: Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_d > 0: total available prosumer surplus is smaller than total demand.","expression":"(E_d > 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left( p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} \\;\\land\\; p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_d","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.3. Scenario 3 (E_d > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is smaller than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, cannot meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency and therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the P2P buying and selling prices become p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} and p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Aggregate surplus E_s is total seller surplus minus total buyer deficit, and E_d is its negative.","expression":"E_s = \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} - \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}; \\quad E_d = -E_s","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_E_s","param_E_d","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g01-E04"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The monetary benefit ν to the group of prosumers can be viewed as the difference between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = p_{g,b}[E_s]^{+} − p_{g,s}[E_d]^{+}, where E_s = ∑_{n∈N_s} E_{n,s} − ∑_{n∈N_b} E_{n,d}, E_d = −E_s, [·]^{+} = max(0, ·), and p_{g,s} and p_{g,b} are respectively selling and buying prices by the grid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The grid selling price is greater than the grid buying price.","expression":"p_{g,s} > p_{g,b}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10"],"ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Now, note that p_{g,s} > p_{g,b} [12], which is due to the fact that retailers pay prosumers less for their excess generation, since it is considered non-dispatchable. Therefore, a suitable P2P trading price can potentially motivate the prosumers to cooperate with one another in order to trade electricity among themselves, if possible, instead of trading with the CPS. To this end, the mid-market rate [57] is proposed to use as the pricing mechanism for this coalition game based P2P trading."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"var_p_s","symbol":"p_s","meaning":"Peer-to-peer selling price per unit of electricity, set by the mid-market pricing rule in the applicable scenario.","kind":"operational","domain":"scalar price per unit of electricity; scenario-determined by the mid-market rate","unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::x::var_p_s"},{"variable_id":"var_p_b","symbol":"p_b","meaning":"Peer-to-peer buying price per unit of electricity, set by the mid-market pricing rule in the applicable scenario.","kind":"operational","domain":"scalar price per unit of electricity; scenario-determined by the mid-market rate","unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::x::var_p_b"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"param_p_gs","symbol":"p_{g,s}","meaning":"Grid selling price per unit of electricity; a reference price input for mid-market P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_p_gs"},{"parameter_id":"param_p_gb","symbol":"p_{g,b}","meaning":"Grid buying price per unit of electricity; a reference price input for mid-market P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_p_gb"},{"parameter_id":"param_E_s","symbol":"E_s","meaning":"Aggregate difference between total prosumer surplus and total demand in the network; used as the scenario condition and formula input for P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_E_s"},{"parameter_id":"param_E_d","symbol":"E_d","meaning":"Negative value of E_s; aggregate deficit-side scenario condition and formula input for P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_E_d"},{"parameter_id":"param_E_ns","symbol":"E_{n,s}","meaning":"Energy surplus of prosumer n; individual surplus input appearing in the seller-side sums of the scenario price formulas.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_E_ns"},{"parameter_id":"param_E_nd","symbol":"E_{n,d}","meaning":"Energy deficiency of prosumer n; individual deficit input appearing in the buyer-side sums of the scenario price formulas.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::theta::param_E_nd"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"30-motivational_game::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"mid_market_residual_weighted_rates","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Unit sale price p_s and unit purchase price p_b are formed according to net surplus or shortfall: when E_s=0 and E_d=0, p_s=p_b=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2; when E_s>0, p_b=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2, p_s=(p_b∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}+p_{g,b}E_s)/∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}; when E_d>0, p_s=(p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2, p_b=(p_s∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}+p_{g,s}E_d)/∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: P2P unit sale price p_s and unit purchase price p_b.","Core operation: arithmetic midpoint of the grid purchase and sale prices and energy-weighted conversion of revenues and expenditures from surplus/shortfall transactions.","Conditional relationship: select the corresponding formula according to supply-demand balance, net surplus, or net shortfall.","Output: p_s and p_b under each case."],"variation_fields":["p_{g,s} is the grid sale price, p_{g,b} is the grid purchase price, and p_{g,s}>p_{g,b}.","E_s=∑_{n∈N_s}E_{n,s}−∑_{n∈N_b}E_{n,d}，E_d=−E_s","N_s and N_b are used to aggregate seller surplus and buyer shortfall, respectively.","Both P2P prices lie in [p_{g,b},p_{g,s}].","Peer-to-peer transactions require p_b\u003cp_{g,s} and p_s>p_{g,b}.","Prices must encourage participation and make the distribution of attainable gains satisfy the coalition core requirements."],"constraint_refs":["30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C005","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C006","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C007","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C008","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","30-motivational_game::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"paper_id":"30-motivational_game","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"CBA"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/280"});
